    "response": " To find the average speed of the car over the interval \\(0 \\leq t \\leq 10\\) seconds, we use the formula for the average value of a function:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average speed} = \\frac{1}{b-a} \\int_a^b v(t) \\, dt\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(a = 0\\), \\(b = 10\\), and \\(v(t) = 3t - \\frac{t^2}{2}\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average speed} = \\frac{1}{10-0} \\int_0^{10} \\left(3t - \\frac{t^2}{2}\\right) \\, dt = \\frac{1}{10} \\int_0^{10} \\left(3t - \\frac{t^2}{2}\\right) \\, dt\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate the integral. We do this by integrating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^{10} \\left(3t - \\frac{t^2}{2}\\right) \\, dt = \\int_0^{10} 3t \\, dt - \\int_0^{10} \\frac{t^2}{2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nThe integral of \\(3t\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^{10} 3t \\, dt = 3 \\left[ \\frac{t^2}{2} \\right]_0^{10} = 3 \\left( \\frac{10^2}{2} - \\frac{0^2}{2} \\right) = 3 \\left( \\frac{100}{2} \\right) = 3 \\cdot 50 = 150\n\\]\n\nThe integral of \\(\\frac{t^2}{2}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^{10} \\frac{t^2}{2} \\, dt = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ \\frac{t^3}{3} \\right]_0^{10} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{10^3}{3} - \\frac{0^3}{3} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{1000}{3} \\right) = \\frac{500}{3}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^{10} \\left(3t - \\frac{t^2}{2}\\right) \\, dt = 150 - \\frac{500}{3} = \\frac{450}{3} - \\frac{500}{3} = -\\frac{50}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we divide by 10 to find the average speed:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average speed} = \\frac{1}{10} \\left( -\\frac{50}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{50}{30} = -\\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince speed is a non-negative quantity, we take the absolute value of the average speed:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average speed} = \\frac{5}{3} \\text{ m/s}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the total distance traveled. The total distance traveled is given by the integral of the absolute value of the velocity function over the interval \\(0 \\leq t \\leq 10\\). First, we need to determine where the velocity function changes s